[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 7"""
 date="2026-10-14T14:24:27Z"
 content="""
How to handle upgrades?

The choices seem to be:

1. Do nothing. All special remotes will be unverified, and so all pushes
   will be prevented until the user verifies them.
2. Register existing special remotes as verified automatically on upgrade. 
   Recommend that the user check the special remotes that they have already
   enabled, which they can do with `git-annex verifyremote`.

The big problem with #1 is that people have automated systems that are
working. If upgrading git-annex requires a manual step to keep them
working, and missing that causes existing data flows stop happening,
it's entirely possible that it will lead to data loss. This makes me
deeply uncomfortable with #1. A fairly low impact security hole should
not lead to a high risk of data loss.

And if someone discovered this security hole already and has been
exploiting it, then they will have probably already obtained all the
annexed objects in the special remote. So #1 feels like locking the door
after the cows are out.

If someone learns about this security hole from the advisory and starts trying
to exploit it, it will be down to a race between them and when git-annex gets
upgraded. But, if a user has just enabled a special remote and then
learns about this security hole, they have an obvious thing to check.

So, I think #2 is the best choice.
"""]]
